WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:06.660
The Boys' lengthy 72-issue, 2006-2012 comics
run means there's plenty of lore, twists,

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:08.940
and characters left out or remixed in the
show.

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:13.000
So in preparation for the coming season and
facts that fans may be unaware of, here's

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:15.520
a complete guide to Amazon's The Boys.

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:20.600
The Boys, based on the comic series of the
same name by Irish comic writer Garth Ennis,

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:24.380
was adapted for the screen by veteran TV writer
and showrunner Eric Kripke.

00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:28.320
Before The Boys, Kripke was most well known
as the showrunner of Supernatural.

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:32.250
At first, the two shows don't seem to have
a lot in common; Supernatural revolves around

00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:36.660
monsters, religion, and other inter-dimensional
beings, and The Boys explores the human flaws

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:37.700
of superheroes.

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:40.140
Still, both shows are distinctly "Kripkeian."

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:45.080
Supernatural found success by poking fun at
genre conventions, and The Boys is no different.

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:49.340
The dynamic between the leads and the way
each episode lovingly mocks superhero tropes

00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:50.700
are the best parts of the show.

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:55.040
For good measure, Kripke is an enormous fan
of the original comic's run and its creator.

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:57.840
In a 2019 interview, Kripke told Collider,

00:00:57.840 --> 00:00:59.320
"I'm a huge Garth Ennis fan.

00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:01.400
I'm a big fan of everything the guy writes."

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:06.100
With a genre-tested fan at the helm, it's
no surprise The Boys has been such a success.

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:10.859
While the creator is an enormous fan of the
series, Eric Kripke has also stated that not

00:01:10.859 --> 00:01:14.609
everything from the comics will make it to
the small screen... which is probably for

00:01:14.609 --> 00:01:15.360
the best.

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:20.200
Ennis' work evaluates the hypocrisy and absurdity
of institutions, beliefs, and symbols that

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:23.300
most societies hold dear, from religion to
superheroes.

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:27.620
However, Ennis often parodies these sacred
ideas with extreme violence, toilet humor,

00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:29.720
and every other aspect of "adult content."

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:31.760
It's not exactly TV-friendly.

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:35.300
In other words, there's a price to getting
the comics adapted for television.

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:36.120
"The price?

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:42.100
Whatever it is, I'll pay it."

00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:46.579
Even with the leniency a platform like Amazon
provides, the comics remain far too graphic

00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:49.160
to ever be entirely faithfully adapted for
TV.

00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:53.520
None of this is to say Kripke plans on watering
down the gonzo spirit of the comics.

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:57.740
Considering everything we saw in season one,
from botched dolphin rescues to an invisible

00:01:57.740 --> 00:02:01.859
man getting a shocking surprise in his rear
end, it seems Kripke has finally gotten to

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:02.860
let loose.

00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:07.620
The audience's surrogate into the world of
The Boys is Hughie Campbell, played by Jack

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:08.380
Quaid.

00:02:08.380 --> 00:02:11.740
Hughie's quest to avenge the death of his
girlfriend, who was accidentally murdered

00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:14.400
by A-Train, is a core part of the show's story.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:18.680
While Quaid does a great job of bringing his
character to life on screen, the role on paper

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:19.960
had already been cast.

00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:24.620
When creating Hughie, Ennis and illustrator
Darick Robertson modeled the character off

00:02:24.620 --> 00:02:25.820
actor Simon Pegg.

00:02:25.820 --> 00:02:29.980
While Pegg has aged out of the part he inspired,
he still appears on the show as Hughie's father,

00:02:29.980 --> 00:02:32.320
in a nod to the work that inspired it all.

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:36.960
While Eric Kripke has remained faithful to
the energy of the original comics, he also

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:40.540
made several creative decisions to reshape
the basic plot of the show.

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:44.599
One of the largest differences between season
one of the show and the comics is the Boys'

00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:45.880
lack of superpowers.

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:50.120
In Ennis' original run, the members of the
team are aware of Compound-V and its power

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:52.400
granting effect from the get-go of the series.

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:56.500
Hughie is injected as soon as he joins the
team and the Boys do some serious damage as

00:02:56.540 --> 00:02:57.860
a result of their abilities.

00:02:57.860 --> 00:03:02.300
They have the compound because, unlike the
TV adaptation, they're backed and funded by

00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:03.160
the CIA.

00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:06.820
Removing that from the television show raises
the stakes on the series.

00:03:06.820 --> 00:03:10.540
The superheroes they're fighting are a much
bigger threat, so the audience empathizes

00:03:10.540 --> 00:03:12.420
and roots for its underdog heroes.

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:16.500
It also has kept diehard fans of the original
run on their toes, because the Boys have less

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:17.460
protection.

00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:22.340
Not to say none of the Boys have power the
Female, Kimiko, played by Karen Fukahara,

00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:26.540
retains her penchant for violence and Compound
V-given powers on the show as well.

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:30.380
Another departure from the comics affects
the structure of the series.

00:03:30.380 --> 00:03:34.459
Ennis' story arcs often revolve around the
Boys infiltrating and blackmailing groups

00:03:34.459 --> 00:03:38.599
less powerful and notable than the Seven in
an effort to find dirt on Vought's superhero

00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:39.500
organization.

00:03:39.500 --> 00:03:43.440
The show, on the other hand, has not introduced
the audience to other fully formed superhero

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:45.020
teams beyond brief mentions.

00:03:45.020 --> 00:03:49.320
While the other superhero teams the Boys encounter
in the comics offer humorous takes on popular

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:52.580
franchises, leaving them out of the show streamlines
the adaptation.

00:03:52.580 --> 00:03:56.120
The individual encounters with each group
wouldn't work as well in The Boys' current

00:03:56.120 --> 00:03:57.400
eight-episode format.

00:03:57.400 --> 00:04:01.840
In the comics, the Boys' battles with different
groups last for issues at a time, often only

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:06.069
resulting in major progression for larger
overarching stories in the final issues of

00:04:06.069 --> 00:04:07.160
a given storyline.

00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:10.880
It's a structure that wouldn't translate well
to the dramatic satire Kripke's producing

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:11.800
onscreen.

00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:15.940
However, Kripke produced plenty of "monster
of the week" episodes on Supernatural, so

00:04:15.940 --> 00:04:19.580
maybe weekly showdowns with new heroes is
in the cards for season two.

00:04:20.220 --> 00:04:24.720
Season two's cast is expanding with additional
characters, including Butcher's best buddy

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:26.800
and slobber-mouthed English bulldog, Terror.

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:29.640
The most notable newcomer is Aya Cash's Stormfront.

00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:34.100
In the comics, Stormfront is a lot like Thor,
with abilities including super-strength, flight,

00:04:34.100 --> 00:04:35.620
and control over lightning.

00:04:35.620 --> 00:04:39.340
Unlike the beloved God of Thunder, however,
Stormfront is also a Nazi.

00:04:39.340 --> 00:04:43.120
There's already been some theorizing on the
internet about how Stormfront's vile beliefs

00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:46.680
will be depicted on the show, especially since
the creators have opted to gender swap the

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:47.660
character.

00:04:47.660 --> 00:04:51.740
Cash hasn't confirmed whether her character
will be a Nazi like her illustrated counterpart

00:04:51.740 --> 00:04:54.240
or if the show will even include that part
of her character.

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:58.720
However, Kripke stated his motivations for
the gender swap during a Cable and Telecommunications

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:01.800
Marketing Association virtual panel, telling
the audience,

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:04.320
"We wanted to sort of create Homelander's
worst nightmare.

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:07.840
And his worst nightmare would be a strong
woman who wasn't afraid of him and proceeded

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:09.120
to steal his spotlight."

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:13.340
Adding a character to the Seven who directly
challenges the authority of its leader Homelander

00:05:13.340 --> 00:05:15.560
is a great way to shake up the second season.

00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:20.360
Season two could potentially expand its narrative
by exploring the lore of The Boys.

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:24.720
In the comics, the fight between the Boys
and the Supes originated in World War II,

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:29.320
when Army Captain Greg Mallory lost his battalion
due to the mistakes of untrained superheroes.

00:05:29.320 --> 00:05:33.060
The Supes were placed on the frontlines by
the Vought Corporation in an effort to gain

00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:35.080
a government contract for their superheroes.

00:05:35.080 --> 00:05:39.880
After WWII, Mallory joined the CIA and made
it his life's mission to monitor the Supes

00:05:39.880 --> 00:05:42.160
and prevent Vought from gaining federal support.

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:45.800
Along the way, he assembled the Boys, starting
the narrative of the comics.

00:05:45.800 --> 00:05:50.900
In the show, Grace Mallory's full origins
remain unexplored and Mallory is also gender-swapped.

00:05:50.900 --> 00:05:55.120
Like the character she was adapted from, Mallory
was a member of the CIA and founder of the

00:05:55.120 --> 00:05:55.700
Boys.

00:05:55.700 --> 00:05:59.840
However, beyond her motivations for hating
the Supes and recruiting Butcher, her actions

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:01.940
before the show remain undisclosed.

00:06:01.940 --> 00:06:05.940
Exploring the original iteration of the team,
before Hughie joined with Mallory still in

00:06:05.940 --> 00:06:10.070
charge, could be a great way for Kripke and
company to continue to flesh out the engaging

00:06:10.070 --> 00:06:11.720
and brutal world they've created.

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:16.860
While he had a surprisingly complex arc in
the first season, the Deep is a very different

00:06:16.860 --> 00:06:18.440
character than his inspiration.

00:06:18.440 --> 00:06:20.240
And we're not talking about Aquaman.

00:06:20.240 --> 00:06:24.600
In Ennis' series, the Deep is a very minor
character, and he generally functions as a

00:06:24.600 --> 00:06:27.800
punchline because he's the only superhero
concerned with accounting.

00:06:27.800 --> 00:06:31.920
However, on the show, the Deep is used to
explore the abuse of power dynamics and sexual

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:32.640
assault.

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:37.100
Season one's first episode addresses one of
the most controversial moments in the comic:

00:06:37.100 --> 00:06:38.480
the assault of Starlight.

00:06:38.480 --> 00:06:42.580
In both the comic and the show, when Starlight
first joins the Seven, she is coerced into

00:06:42.580 --> 00:06:43.560
sexual acts.

00:06:43.560 --> 00:06:48.080
Kripke and his creative team wisely decided
to take the moment and its consequences seriously

00:06:48.080 --> 00:06:51.890
for its female lead and follow her as she
gains the strength to call out The Deep.

00:06:51.890 --> 00:06:55.910
Making the Deep a larger part allowed the
storyline the space it needed to be handled

00:06:55.910 --> 00:06:58.160
seriously, not just for shock value.

00:06:58.760 --> 00:07:02.460
Much has been made of Karl Urban's strong
performance as Billy Butcher.

00:07:02.460 --> 00:07:06.600
Considering Urban's long career in beloved
comics adaptations, it makes sense that the

00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:08.800
actor crafted such a strong performance.

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:13.800
Before Butcher, Urban played a CIA agent in
the adaptation of Warren Ellis' Red, Skurge

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:17.780
the Executioner in Thor: Ragnarok, and he
brought his grizzled chin to the definitive

00:07:17.780 --> 00:07:19.770
onscreen version of Judge Dredd.

00:07:20.100 --> 00:07:21.240
"I am the law."

00:07:21.660 --> 00:07:26.060
Beyond his roles in successful comics adaptations,
Urban has also made a career out of appearing

00:07:26.060 --> 00:07:28.480
as a character actor in sci-fi franchises.

00:07:28.480 --> 00:07:32.840
He played the villainous Vaako in the Riddick
series, and he appeared in the Doom adaptation

00:07:32.840 --> 00:07:34.320
alongside Dwayne Johnson.

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:38.720
He even nailed the stiff mannerisms of Star
Trek's Dr. Bones in J.J. Abrams' franchise

00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:39.520
reboot.

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:43.840
All this is to say that Urban's a genre legend,
and The Boys is made all the better by giving

00:07:43.840 --> 00:07:46.180
this veteran character actor center stage.

00:07:46.600 --> 00:07:51.320
Karl Urban's not the only actor on The Boys
with a pulpy genre background the man portraying

00:07:51.320 --> 00:07:54.000
his nemesis got his career started the same
way.

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:58.420
Anthony Starr, whose Homelander performance
drew positive notice for the suppressed insanity

00:07:58.420 --> 00:08:02.840
he brings to scenes, got his major breakthrough
in another not-for-network-TV enterprise called

00:08:02.840 --> 00:08:03.700
Banshee.

00:08:03.700 --> 00:08:07.480
It tells the story of an escaped criminal,
played by Starr, who assumes the identity

00:08:07.480 --> 00:08:10.140
of small-town Pennsylvania sheriff Lucas Hood.

00:08:10.140 --> 00:08:14.500
Following Hood's exploits as a criminal sheriff,
it's one of the most bonkers shows ever committed

00:08:14.500 --> 00:08:15.580
to the small screen.

00:08:15.580 --> 00:08:20.840
The show delivered ludicrous storylines involving
the Russian mafia, CIA, and a particularly

00:08:20.840 --> 00:08:23.210
nasty excommunicated Amish drug dealer.

00:08:23.210 --> 00:08:27.650
However, it also featured some of the best
fight scenes on TV before or since, and a

00:08:27.650 --> 00:08:31.640
stellar central performance from Starr, who
wisely chose to play Hood against type.

00:08:31.640 --> 00:08:36.260
Instead of being a zany criminal or a strong
and silent warrior, Hood is a man barely clinging

00:08:36.260 --> 00:08:38.820
to reality due to the trauma he's experienced.

00:08:38.820 --> 00:08:43.700
Each scene he's in plays like an interaction
with a caged animal he's defensive, on edge,

00:08:43.710 --> 00:08:47.240
and clearly fighting against the circumstances
presented by his current situation.

00:08:47.240 --> 00:08:50.860
That experience has worked wonders for him
and Kripke on The Boys.

00:08:51.280 --> 00:08:55.300
While the comics have plenty of plot twists
that the show has yet to reveal, Kripke already

00:08:55.300 --> 00:08:58.260
surprised fans of The Boys with the ending
of season one.

00:08:58.260 --> 00:09:02.960
Spoiler ahead: All season, Butcher, Hughie,
and the rest of the black ops couch surfers

00:09:02.960 --> 00:09:06.340
operate under the assumption that Butcher
is avenging his wife's death.

00:09:06.340 --> 00:09:10.180
In Ennis' run, Butcher's wife was assaulted
and murdered by one of The Seven.

00:09:10.180 --> 00:09:14.120
However, in the show, Kripke pulled the rug
out from under his audience: In the final

00:09:14.130 --> 00:09:18.230
moments of the first season, it is revealed
Butcher's wife Rebecca is still alive and

00:09:18.230 --> 00:09:19.600
has Homelander's son.

00:09:19.600 --> 00:09:23.500
Both Homelander and Butcher were led to believe
Rebecca had perished, and their discovery

00:09:23.500 --> 00:09:25.420
upends their arcs in different ways.

00:09:25.420 --> 00:09:30.560
Homelander murders his handler, Madelyn Stillwell,
and Butcher realizes his sole reason for fighting

00:09:30.560 --> 00:09:31.600
the Seven was a lie.

00:09:31.600 --> 00:09:35.720
Both characters are left adrift by the revelation,
and fans were left clamoring to see the next

00:09:35.720 --> 00:09:36.500
season.

00:09:36.500 --> 00:09:40.820
They won't have to wait long; season 2 of
The Boys premieres on Amazon Prime on September

00:09:40.820 --> 00:09:41.320
4.

00:09:41.500 --> 00:09:43.060
"This is never gonna stop.

00:09:43.060 --> 00:09:46.100
It's just gonna be more blood and awfulness."

00:09:46.680 --> 00:09:49.720
"That's the f--king game"

